course: world
lesson: 188
title: Check the claim, not the person
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A claim is true or false all by itself: two plus two is four whether a professor says it, a six-year-old says it, or your most annoying cousin says it.
explain: So when someone answers an idea with "You're six, what would you know?", "You don't even recycle at home" or "You're not my friend", they have dodged the idea and hit the person. Grown-ups call it attacking the person (ad hominem).
explain: Tom tries it on his little sister. Mum: "Is she wrong, Tom? Two plus two? Go on, check." Tom: "...It's four."
explain: It works the other way too: having a scientist for a mum doesn't make Ava right about the weather. Being related to an expert, or being popular, isn't evidence.
explain: The fair way is always the same: ask "Is the claim true? What are the reasons?" and leave the person's age, looks, habits and friends out of it.
fact: A true thing is true whoever says it, even a little kid.
fact: Attacking the person instead of the claim is called ad hominem.
fact: Pointing out that someone doesn't do what they say ("you too") may show they are unfair, but it doesn't make their idea wrong.
fact: The Moon's gravity pulling on the oceans is the main cause of the tides; the Sun causes smaller tides too.
fact: Good posture can help your back feel better, especially if you sit for a long time, and moving around often matters even more.
fact: Being related to an expert doesn't make you one, and an expert on frogs isn't an expert on weather.
fact: Chocolate is poisonous to dogs because it contains theobromine, which dogs can't handle well.
fact: Studies show that play and exercise during the day can help children concentrate.
fact: Small slips in speaking don't change the facts; judge a project on its facts.
fact: Some zoos run breeding programmes that have helped animals such as the Arabian oryx and the California condor, though people still argue about zoos.
fact: The Earth goes round the Sun once a year.
fact: Good ideas can come from anyone, whether or not they are your friend.
fact: Words people use: "spot on" means exactly right; "sound" (Liverpool, Manchester) means good, reliable; "Scouse" means from Liverpool; "la" (Liverpool) means mate; "dead" (Liverpool) means very; "delulu" (young people) means believing something that isn't real; "skibidi" is a word kids shout that means nothing; "do the needful" (Indian English) means do what needs doing; "babe" is a friendly word between friends.
exercise: Tom's little sister: "Two plus two is four." Tom: "You're six, what would you know?" What is wrong with Tom's reply? | Her age doesn't change the maths | Six-year-olds can't add up properly yet | Tom is older, so he's right | Two plus two is really five
exercise: Ella: "We should recycle more at school." Kai: "You don't even recycle at home, so your idea's rubbish." Is Ella's idea wrong because of that? | No; the idea can still be good | Yes; only perfect people have good ideas | Yes; ideas from home don't count | No, because recycling is always wrong
exercise: What is the "you don't do it either" trick called? | "You too" (tu quoque) | A straw man | A slippery slope argument | A red herring
exercise: Ben says the Moon causes the tides. Josh: "Ben's bad at football, so he doesn't know." What mainly causes the tides? | The pull of the Moon's gravity | Wind blowing hard across the sea | Big ships pushing the water about | Rain filling up the sea every day
exercise: Grandma: "Sit up straight, it's better for your back." Leo: "You're old, you don't know about now." What is fair? | Check the claim; good posture and moving about help backs | Old people's advice is always wrong | Old people's advice is always right | Backs don't care how you sit
exercise: Ava's friend: "Ava's mum is a scientist, so whatever Ava says about the weather must be right." What is wrong? | Ava isn't her mum, and an expert in one thing isn't in everything | Scientists never talk about the weather | Ava is too young to talk about anything | Ava's mum must be a weather expert
exercise: Sam: "Chocolate is bad for dogs." Max: "You've never even had a dog." Is Sam right? | Yes; chocolate is poisonous to dogs | No; only dog owners can know | No; dogs can eat chocolate safely in small bits | Yes, but only white chocolate
exercise: What makes chocolate dangerous for dogs? | Theobromine, which dogs can't handle well | The sugar, which makes them hyper and sick | The wrapper, which they swallow | The milk, which they can't drink
exercise: Mia argues for a longer break. Oliver: "You only want that because you're lazy." What is the fair reply? | Answer her reasons, not her motive | Agree she's lazy and drop the whole idea | Call Oliver lazy back | Ask for a shorter break instead
exercise: Amir says one word slightly wrong in his talk. A boy: "He can't even say the words, so his whole project is wrong." What is fair? | Judge the project on its facts | Throw out any project with a slip | Mark the project on speaking only | Make Amir say the word ten times
exercise: Lucy: "Zoos can help save endangered animals." Ethan: "Lucy cried at the zoo once, so she can't talk about zoos." Which animals have zoo breeding programmes helped? | The Arabian oryx and the California condor | The dodo and the woolly mammoth, many years ago | The house sparrow and the pigeon | The T. rex and the sabre-toothed cat
exercise: Chloe: "My brother told me the Earth goes round the Sun." Her friend: "Your brother is annoying, so he's wrong." What is true? | The Earth goes round the Sun; annoying people can be right | The Sun goes round the Earth | Annoying people are always wrong | Brothers are never right about space
exercise: Freddie: "Let's put the pictures on the left." Jade: "Your idea's bad because you're not my friend." What should decide? | What makes the project better | Who is friends with whom | Who shouts first | Who has the most stickers on their chart
exercise: What do grown-ups call attacking the person instead of the claim? | Ad hominem | Correlation | The Monty Hall problem | A fair test
exercise: Which question gets to the point fastest? | Is the claim true? | Who said it? | How old are they, really? | Do I like them?
test: Mum: "Is she wrong, Tom? Two plus two? Go on, check." Tom: "...It's four." What did Mum teach him? | Check the claim, not the age of whoever said it | Little kids are always right | Big brothers should never check | Maths changes as you get older
test: The teacher: "Maybe Ella should recycle at home too. But is her idea for school good or not?" What is the teacher separating? | The person's habits from the idea | Home rubbish from school rubbish bins | Ella from Kai, so they stop arguing | Paper from plastic in the recycling
test: A Scouse mate: "Ben's football is dead bad, la, but he's sound on the Moon." Is he reasoning well? | Yes; football has nothing to do with tides | No; bad footballers know nothing | No; nobody knows what causes tides | Yes, but only because Ben is his mate from school
test: Mum: "Grandma's been sitting for 75 years. She's basically a sitting expert." Joke aside, how should you judge Grandma's advice? | Check the claim against the evidence | Accept it because she is old | Reject it because she is old | Ask the youngest person in the house instead
test: Ava: "My mum studies frogs, not weather. And I'm not my mum, babe." What is she pointing out? | Borrowed expertise doesn't make her a weather expert | Frogs predict the weather perfectly | Her mum is really a weather scientist | Children can't talk about weather
test: Sam: "I've also never had a lion. Still know not to hug one, mate." What is his point? | You can know facts without owning the animal | Lions are the same as dogs | Hugging a lion is safe if you own one yourself | Only owners can know about animals
test: Mia: "Maybe I do want it, Oliver, but did you hear my reasons? Kids need to run around." What should Oliver do now? | Answer her reasons | Call her lazy again, louder | Change the subject to football | Walk off without a word
test: The teacher: "His facts were spot on. You might want to check yours before you check his words." What does the teacher mean? | A small slip doesn't make the facts wrong | One wrong word makes the whole project wrong | Speaking matters more than facts | Nobody should ever correct a word
test: What would happen if everyone judged ideas only by who said them? | Good ideas from unpopular people would be thrown away | Only true ideas would survive | Arguments would get much fairer | Nothing at all would change
test: A kid, full delulu: "Your bro's annoying, so the Earth doesn't go round the Sun. Skibidi." What is true? | Annoying or not, the brother is right about the Earth | Annoying brothers make the Earth stop | "Skibidi" proves the Sun moves round us | Nobody knows what goes round what
test: The teacher: "Crying at a zoo doesn't cancel facts, Ethan. Answer her point." Which point should Ethan answer? | Some zoos help save endangered animals | Lucy cries too easily | All zoos should close today and for ever | Ethan likes the zoo too
test: A teacher in Delhi: "Pictures left or right? Do the needful, children, not the friend list." What should decide? | What makes the project better | Who is friends with whom | Who has known whom the longest | Who sits nearest the teacher
exam: Identify the fallacy committed where a claim is rejected by reason of the age of the person advancing it. | Attacking the person rather than the claim | A false dilemma | The fence-post error | An appeal to tradition, since the old know best
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the tides of the oceans. | The gravitational attraction of the Moon | The prevailing winds over the oceans of the world | The passage of large vessels | The rainfall upon the oceans
exam: It is given that a pupil who advocates recycling at school does not recycle at home. Select the response consistent with sound reasoning. | The merit of the proposal must be judged independently of the pupil's conduct | The proposal is thereby shown to be without merit | The proposal is thereby shown to be meritorious | The pupil is thereby prohibited from making proposals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the substance which renders chocolate poisonous to dogs? | Theobromine, which dogs metabolise poorly | Sugar, which renders dogs excitable | Milk, which dogs are unable to drink | Cocoa butter, which dogs cannot swallow or digest
